Here it is concretely. Implementers would write only this:

```typescript
interface BrainStore {
  getEntity(id: EntityId): Promise<EntityRef | null>;
  getFact(id: string): Promise<Fact | null>;
  factsBySubject(id: EntityId, page: PageReq): Promise<Page<Fact>>;
  factsByObject(id: EntityId, page: PageReq): Promise<Page<Fact>>;
  factsCiting(factId: string): Promise<Fact[]>;   // facts whose sources include this fact
  search(text: string, embedding: number[], limit: number): Promise<Fact[]>;
  commit(batch: { writer: WriterId; rationale: string; entities: EntityRef[]; facts: Fact[] })
    : Promise<{ revision: string }>;              // atomic: all or nothing
}
```

Jive's shared core implements the six agent-facing methods on top of `BrainStore`. That includes freshness, quote checks, contradictions, the ownership rule, and the code layer. The git-repo version and a later Postgres version would each be one `BrainStore` implementation.

You're right. Here's the complete version:

```typescript
type EntityId = string;      // stable, opaque: "service/rate-engine", "scip:<symbol>"
type FactId = string;
type WriterId = string;      // "claude/dat-reader@1"
type Timestamp = string;     // ISO 8601, UTC
type Json = null | boolean | number | string | Json[] | { [k: string]: Json };

interface EntityRef {
  id: EntityId;
  type: string;              // "service", "person", "customer", ...
  name: string;
  aliases: string[];
}

type Source = { at?: Timestamp; quote?: string } & (
  | { type: 'git'; repo: string; path: string; commit: string; blob: string; lines?: [number, number] }
  | { type: 'symbol'; symbol: string; repo: string; commit: string; blob: string }
  | { type: 'doc' | 'message' | 'meeting' | 'artifact'; ref: string; version: string }
  | { type: 'fact'; factId: FactId; revision: string }
);

interface Fact {
  id: FactId;
  subject: EntityId;
  predicate: string;
  object?: EntityId;         // exactly one of object / value
  value?: Json;
  basis: 'compiler-derived' | 'imported' | 'human-asserted' | 'model-inferred';
  writer: WriterId;
  recordedAt: Timestamp;
  revision: string;          // changes on every edit or retraction
  sources: Source[];         // length >= 1
  contradicts: FactId[];
  status: 'active' | 'retracted';
  retractedReason?: string;
}

interface PageReq { limit: number; cursor?: string }
interface Page<T> { items: T[]; nextCursor?: string }

interface BrainStore {
  getEntity(id: EntityId): Promise<EntityRef | null>;
  entitiesByName(nameOrAlias: string): Promise<EntityRef[]>;          // exact, case-insensitive
  getFact(id: FactId): Promise<Fact | null>;
  factsBySubject(id: EntityId, page: PageReq): Promise<Page<Fact>>;
  factsByObject(id: EntityId, page: PageReq): Promise<Page<Fact>>;
  factsCiting(id: FactId): Promise<Fact[]>;                           // facts with a 'fact' source pointing at id
  factsContradicting(id: FactId): Promise<Fact[]>;                    // facts whose contradicts includes id
  searchText(text: string, limit: number): Promise<FactId[]>;         // ranked best first
  searchVector(embedding: number[], limit: number): Promise<FactId[]>;// ranked by cosine similarity
  commit(batch: {
    writer: WriterId;
    rationale: string;
    entities: EntityRef[];
    facts: Fact[];
    embeddings: Record<FactId, number[]>;                             // computed by the core
  }): Promise<{ revision: string; recordedAt: Timestamp }>;           // atomic: all or nothing
}
```

There are two changes from my last message. Search is split into `searchText` and `searchVector`, and the core merges the results. I also added `entitiesByName` for exact lookup and `factsContradicting`.

